# Project One Get-Item Pseudocode
#
# Graded checkpoint: Module Five Project One
# Later use: get-item and inventory design for M7 Project Two
#
# Review the course-provided get-item flowchart before completing this file.
# Write pseudocode, not Python code. Use clear indentation and course-appropriate
# keywords such as INPUT, OUTPUT, IF, ELSE, SET, ADD, or similar terms.

START get_item

    # TODO 1: Identify the information the get-item process needs.
    # Consider the current room, item in that room, and player's inventory.

    # Information needed:
    #   current_room  - the name of the room the player is in
    #   room_item     - the item located in current_room, or "none" if the
    #                   room has no item or the item was already collected
    #   inventory     - the list of items the player has collected so far
    #                   (starts empty)
    #   command       - the get-item command the player types
    #                   (format: "get <item name>")
    #   requested_item - the item name taken from the command
    #
    # Room items (matches the Map of Viltrum):
    #   Main Hub            - none (start room)
    #   Science Laboratory  - Data Stick
    #   Observation Tower   - Navigation Map
    #   Crew Quarters       - Keycard
    #   Medical Bay         - Bandage
    #   Maintenance Room    - Battery Cell
    #   Weapon Room         - GreatSword
    #   Throne Room         - none (villain room)

    # TODO 2: Prompt for and receive the player's get-item command.

    OUTPUT "Enter a command (example: get Keycard):"
    INPUT command
    SET requested_item TO the item name that follows "get" in command

    # TODO 3: Validate whether the requested item is available in the current
    # room. Show the decision clearly.

    IF requested_item is equal to room_item of current_room
        # valid request, see TODO 4
    ELSE
        # invalid request, see TODO 5

    # TODO 4: Describe what happens when the item request is valid.
    # Include the inventory update and player output.

    IF requested_item is equal to room_item of current_room
        ADD requested_item TO inventory
        SET room_item of current_room TO "none"
        OUTPUT "You picked up the " + requested_item + "."
        OUTPUT "Inventory: " + inventory

    # TODO 5: Describe what happens when the item request is invalid.
    # Include the output the player should receive.

    ELSE
        # inventory does NOT change
        OUTPUT "There is no " + requested_item + " in this room."
        OUTPUT "Inventory: " + inventory

    # TODO 6: Show how the process returns to or continues the larger gameplay
    # loop after the command is handled.

    # After the item request is handled (valid or invalid), control returns
    # to the main gameplay loop. The game then displays the current room
    # and asks for the next command (move, get item, or Exit). The loop
    # keeps running until the player exits or reaches a required ending.
    RETURN to the main gameplay loop

END get_item


